{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<h2>Enable docs</h2>
<pre><code>r.UseOpenapiDocs(true)</code></pre>
<p>Call it before the routes and groups it is meant for: docs are recorded when a route is registered, and groups take the setting when they are made.</p>

<h2>Attach route metadata</h2>
<pre><code>r.Get("/users/{id}", showUser, router.Docs{
    Tags:    []string{"users"},
    Summary: "Show user",
    Out: map[string]router.DocOut{
        "200": {
            ApplicationType: "application/json",
            Description:     "OK",
            Object:          User{},
        },
    },
})</code></pre>

<p>The OpenAPI helpers are intentionally lightweight. They are useful for route-level documentation and schema generation without pulling the router away from <code>net/http</code>.</p>

<h2>Read the document</h2>
<pre><code>doc := r.OpenAPI()</code></pre>
<p>The paths of mounted routers appear below their mount path, with their trailing slash. With docs on, the router adds an <code>OPTIONS</code> route for each documented path on the first request, except for paths with an <code>OPTIONS</code> route of their own. Docs are off until <code>UseOpenapiDocs(true)</code>, with <code>New</code> and <code>NewSite</code> alike.</p>

<h2>Servers</h2>
<pre><code>r.AddServerEndpoint("https://api.example.com", "Production")</code></pre>
<p><code>AddServerEndpoint</code> adds a server the API is reached at to the document.</p>
</article>

